# The --json document.  Every kind of command that contributes something
# to it is here: an outcome, a listing, properties, headers, a lock, a
# path, and both shapes of failure -- one from a request and one from a
# refusal that never made one.  The transcript is the document itself,
# pretty-printed by tests/normalise.py, which also fails the session
# outright if it is not well-formed JSON.
lcd @WORK@
mkcol js
cd js
pwd
lpwd
put hello.txt
put second.txt
ls
head hello.txt
propget hello.txt
delete nosuchresource
rmcol hello.txt
mput hello.txt second.txt
# The three commands --json refuses, because each writes a byte stream
# to standard output or runs a program that might.
cat hello.txt
less hello.txt
edit hello.txt
lock hello.txt
showlocks
unlock hello.txt
cd ..
rmcol js
quit
